Keep layering_check / module-map flags out of ThinLTO backend compiles.

`compile_actions` includes `lto_backend`, so these args also land on every
CcLtoBackendCompile action, where they are meaningless (the input is LLVM IR).
Worse, they make the command line of a "shared non-LTO backend" action depend
on whether the rule that created it had `layering_check`/`module_maps`
enabled. Bazel dedups those shareable actions only when their command lines
are identical, so a cc_library built with layering_check and a
cc_shared_library wrapping it without it (or a rules_rust rust_library, which
hardcodes module_maps as unsupported) both registering a backend for the same
bitcode object fail analysis with "is generated by these conflicting actions"
under --features=thin_lto (e.g. @bullet//:BulletCollision vs
@rosdistro//cc:bullet-BulletCollision with --config=portable). Scope the args
to `source_compile_actions`, which rules_cc defines as `compile_actions` minus
`lto_backend` for exactly this reason, matching the legacy unix toolchain
where layering_check never applied to lto-backend.

--- a/cc/toolchains/args/layering_check/BUILD
+++ b/cc/toolchains/args/layering_check/BUILD
@@ -47,7 +47,7 @@
 
 cc_args(
     name = "layering_check_args",
-    actions = ["@rules_cc//cc/toolchains/actions:compile_actions"],
+    actions = ["@rules_cc//cc/toolchains/actions:source_compile_actions"],
     args = [
         "-fmodules-strict-decluse",
         "-Wprivate-header",
@@ -56,7 +56,7 @@
 
 cc_args(
     name = "module_name_args",
-    actions = ["@rules_cc//cc/toolchains/actions:compile_actions"],
+    actions = ["@rules_cc//cc/toolchains/actions:source_compile_actions"],
     args = select({
         "//cc/settings:apple_constraint": ["-Xclang"],
         "//conditions:default": [],
@@ -69,7 +69,7 @@
 
